#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055543} :activity/id #uuid "f340cd26-ce61-5815-8567-bd1eec9dd831" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055969 :tutorial/content "Recall that logarithms that share a common base $n$ obey the following three laws:\n\n- **The Product Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) + \\log_n(b) = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\n- **The Quotient Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\n- **The Power Rule**\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can use these laws together to simplify a logarithmic expression.\n\nFor instance, we can write $3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y)$ as an expression containing a single term.\n\nFirst, using the power rule, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y) &= \\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2\\left(y^{1/ 2}\\right)\\\\\n\t&= \\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2 \\left(\\sqrt{y}\\right)\t.\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\log_2\\left(x^3\\right) + \\log_2 \\left(\\sqrt{y}\\right) = \\log_2 \\left(x^3\\sqrt{y}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_2(x) + \\dfrac 1 2 \\log_2(y) = \\log_2 \\left(x^3\\sqrt{y}\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "435a21a1-8874-500c-93b0-c742ac5f2a73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2796 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cdc68959-41d1-54af-880a-9fe77d7686e4" :step/math-academy-id 14488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054537} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4dc94edb-007d-5fe5-b9ca-9ed754adc457" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656359} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055463} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218654